   "subject": "Model fit indices",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Good evening, </font><div><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Just a quick question in regards to the model fit of common factor models without SNP effects. Do the typical standards of SRMR &lt; .05 and CFI &gt; .95 still apply? I ask as the GitHub example had values outside of these guidelines, as well as my own common factor results which I was able to run without any convergence warnings. </font></span><br></div></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Best,</font></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Moreh</font></span></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "aS_LStFpAgAJ",
   "from": "Elliot Tucker-Drob",
   "author": "0b5254eeb9973d7a",
   "date": "2020-08-13T00:16:07Z",
   "subject": "Re: Model fit indices",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div><div dir=\"auto\">In Grotzinger et al we write: </div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">“<span style=\"font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;font-size:18px;color:rgb(34,34,34)\">SRMR values below 0.10 indicate acceptable fit, values less than 0.05 indicate good fit, and a value of 0 indicates perfect fit</span><span style=\"vertical-align:baseline;font-size:13.5px;line-height:0;font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;color:rgb(34,34,34)\"><a title=\"Bentler, P. M. &amp; Hu, L. T. in Structural Equation Modeling: Concepts, Issues, and Applications 76–99 (SAGE Publications Inc., 1995).\" href=\"https://www-nature-com.ezproxy.lib.utexas.edu/articles/s41562-019-0566-x#ref-CR54\" style=\"vertical-align:baseline;text-decoration:none;text-decoration-skip:objects;word-wrap:break-word;word-break:break-word;font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;color:rgb(0,102,153)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www-nature-com.ezproxy.lib.utexas.edu/articles/s41562-019-0566-x%23ref-CR54&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793237813229000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1E9xnmev5sHZP37rmCHog0\">54</a></span><span style=\"font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;font-size:18px;color:rgb(34,34,34)\">.”</span></div><div dir=\"auto\"><span style=\"font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;font-size:18px;color:rgb(34,34,34)\"><br></span></div><div dir=\"auto\"><span style=\"font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;font-size:18px;color:rgb(34,34,34)\">“</span><span style=\"font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;font-size:18px;color:rgb(34,34,34)\"> CFI values of ≥0.90 are typically considered acceptable fit, and values of ≥0.95 are typically considered good model fit</span><span style=\"vertical-align:baseline;font-size:13.5px;line-height:0;font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;color:rgb(34,34,34)\"><a title=\"Kaplan, D. Structural Equation Modeling: Foundations and Extensions Vol. 10 (Sage, 2008).\" href=\"https://www-nature-com.ezproxy.lib.utexas.edu/articles/s41562-019-0566-x#ref-CR51\" style=\"vertical-align:baseline;text-decoration:none;text-decoration-skip:objects;word-wrap:break-word;word-break:break-word;font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;color:rgb(0,102,153)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www-nature-com.ezproxy.lib.utexas.edu/articles/s41562-019-0566-x%23ref-CR51&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793237813229000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3mCBDIAExQb20e0g9kvhU5\">51</a>”</span></div><div dir=\"auto\"><span style=\"vertical-align:baseline;font-size:13.5px;line-height:0;font-family:Harding,Palatino,serif;color:rgb(34,34,34)\"><br></span></div></div><div><div dir=\"auto\">These are rules of thumb, not strict cut offs. You can read more about fit indices in texts such as Kline, or by searching for articles in google scholar. </div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
